  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:tIMhDEVghPc",
    "purpose": "A basics guide to staying warm outdoors: how to layer (base, mid, outer, plus mittens and hats), how outerwear fabrics and insulation work (nylon, wool, down, Gore-Tex), and a simple system for choosing a winter jacket.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "Being too cold or too hot outside usually comes from your layering, not your outer jacket. Start from the simplest jacket that works with a good layering system, and understand fabrics, fill power and construction so you don't chase new gear for its own sake.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Layering has three parts: a base layer (wicking, breathable), a mid layer (warmth, e.g. fleece or a wool sweater) and an outer layer.",
      "Synthetic base layers dry fast but can stink sooner; wool insulates when wet and resists odor but is more fragile, so blends are often recommended.",
      "Mittens, hats, balaclavas and gaiters follow the same layering logic.",
      "Nylon shells are strong for their weight, but heavier nylon lasts longer; in wool jackets the wool itself is the insulation.",
      "Brand fabric weights (e.g. Filson vs Weather Wool) aren't measured the same way, and fabric weight doesn't map directly to warmth.",
      "Fill power measures how much space an ounce of down takes up; fill weight and baffle construction (sewn-through, box, offset, woven or bonded) also matter, and humidity reduces high-fill-power performance.",
      "Treated down (e.g. DownTek) holds up better in wet weather than its reputation suggests; Gore-Tex membranes need DWR re-treatment and can delaminate.",
      "Double-weave baffled jackets, like the L.L.Bean one shown, need no stitching, so they are more wind resistant, don't leak down and can stretch.",
      "The winter jacket selection system: check what layers you have, note your use and climate, take a one-hour test walk, then start from the simplest jacket and add only what you need. Consider vintage, and check repair and warranty policies."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Intro",
        "summary": "Introduces a modern baffled down jacket, a B6 bomber and a 1962 Eddie Bauer K2 parka, and sets up the video as a basics guide and a 'winter jacket selection system'.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Layering and base layers",
        "summary": "Explains that layering is usually behind feeling too hot or too cold, outlines base, mid and outer layers, and compares synthetic, wool, blended and mesh base layers.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Mid layers, mittens and hats",
        "summary": "Covers fleece and wool sweater mid layers, how fleece weight and stitch (jersey vs thermal knit) affect loft, and extends layering to socks, boots, chopper mittens, beanies, balaclavas and gaiters.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Outerwear: nylon and wool",
        "summary": "Compares nylon shells with wool outerwear, recommends wool jacket brands including the Iron Snail Mammoth, and stresses layering under wool.",
        "first_segment": 8,
        "last_segment": 11
      },
      {
        "title": "Down: warmth ratings, fill power and construction",
        "summary": "Explains why warmth is hard to rate, inconsistent fabric weight claims, fill power vs fill weight, humidity effects and baffle construction types.",
        "first_segment": 11,
        "last_segment": 13
      },
      {
        "title": "Down in the wet, Gore-Tex and new tech",
        "summary": "Discusses down's wet-weather reputation, treated down, Gore-Tex membranes (warranty, the banned original formula, delamination, DWR upkeep), future tech like aerogel, and the double-weave baffled L.L.Bean jacket.",
        "first_segment": 14,
        "last_segment": 18
      },
      {
        "title": "Winter jacket selection system",
        "summary": "A step-by-step method for choosing a jacket: assess your layers, note your use and climate, do a test walk, start simple and build up, consider vintage on eBay, and check repair and warranty policies.",
        "first_segment": 18,
        "last_segment": 21
      }
    ],
